<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Middle Kingdom of Egypt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2055–1650 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century-bce">21st century BCE</a> · Also known as Middle Kingdom Egypt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Middle Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was the period of reunified rule that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a>, beginning when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-ii">Mentuhotep II</a> of the Eleventh Dynasty reunited the country. Under the Twelfth Dynasty, with kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senusret-iii">Senusret III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>, Egypt conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-nubia">Lower Nubia</a>, developed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faiyum">Faiyum</a> and traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>. It is regarded as the classical age of Egyptian literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/middle-kingdom-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Middle Kingdom of Egypt" width="320" height="371" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gert7 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Egyptian_old_and_middle_kingdom.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Middle Kingdom, also called the Period of Reunification, followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-intermediate-period">First Intermediate Period</a>, when Old Kingdom power had collapsed and the country was decentralised. Towards the end of that period two rival dynasties, the Tenth at Herakleopolis ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a> and the Theban Eleventh ruling the south from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-cataract">First Cataract</a> to the Tenth Nome of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, fought for control.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-ii">Mentuhotep II</a> took the Theban throne in 2055 BCE and in his fourteenth regnal year used a revolt in the Thinite Nome to attack Herakleopolis, which put up little resistance. He finished consolidating the country by his 39th year, and is regarded as the founder of the period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mentuhotep-ii">Mentuhotep II</a> campaigned as far south as the Second Cataract, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, restored Egyptian hegemony over Sinai and depicted himself as a god in his lifetime, wearing the headdresses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a> and Min. He reigned 51 years. The Eleventh Dynasty ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. The Twelfth, whose kings ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisht">el-Lisht</a>, began with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-i">Amenemhat I</a>, who had to campaign in the Delta, built the Walls of the Ruler in the east Delta and founded a new northern capital, Amenemhet It Tawy.</p><p>The Prophecy of Neferty, dating from about this time, presents a king arising from the far south to restore the kingdom. From the Twelfth Dynasty pharaohs kept standing armies with Nubian contingents, but the kingdom's strategy was defensive, with fortresses at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-cataract">First Cataract</a>, in the Delta and across the Sinai Isthmus.</p><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobekneferu">Sobekneferu</a> came a series of short-lived kings, the first of the Thirteenth Dynasty, whose members were mostly unrelated and are attested on few monuments. The period was followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-intermediate-period">Second Intermediate Period</a>, in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyksos">Hyksos</a> of West Asia ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Eleventh Dynasty rebuilt a centralised administration under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, sometimes divided between north and south, with the Overseer of Sealed Goods as treasurer and the Overseer of the Estate as chief steward. Provinces stayed in the hands of nomarchs from powerful families. High <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> floods, averaging 19 metres above non-flood levels, accompanied the most prosperous years, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenemhat-iii">Amenemhat III</a>.</p><p>Royal pyramids were built of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mud brick</a> with a casing of Tura limestone, and statuary and sarcophagi reached a peak of quality. Literature written for entertainment and reflection appeared in the early Twelfth Dynasty, among it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tale-of-the-shipwrecked-sailor">Tale of the Shipwrecked Sailor</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tale-of-sinuhe">Story of Sinuhe</a> and the Dispute between a man and his Ba, which later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> counted as classic.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Middle Kingdom of Egypt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Egyptian objects of the <b>Middle Kingdom</b> found at the site indicate early contact with Egypt as well.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ugarit">Kingdom of Ugarit · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Middle Kingdom</b> the Coffin Texts, written inside the coffins of officials and nobles, extended such spells to a wider elite.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>…into five stages: Old Egyptian, from about 2700 to 2000 BCE; Middle Egyptian, the classical language of the <b>Middle Kingdom</b>, used for inscriptions until the end of antiquity; Late Egyptian, the language of the New Kingdom from about 1350 BCE; Demotic, from the 7th century BCE; and Coptic,…</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Middle Kingdom</b>, Egypt campaigned in Lower Nubia, possibly to check Kerma, and Senusret I built forts at Ikkur, Quban, Aniba, Buhen and Kor to guard the border and the gold mines of Wadi Allaqi.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kerma-culture">Kerma culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Middle Kingdom</b> the Coffin Texts appeared, in a newer form of the language, with new spells and, for the first time, illustrations.</blockquote><a href="/work/book-of-the-dead">Book of the Dead · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The pyramid was likely looted as early as the First Intermediate Period, and Joyce Tyldesley notes evidence that it had been opened and emptied by the <b>Middle Kingdom</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/great-pyramid-of-giza">Great Pyramid of Giza · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
